   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.2. Increased capacity of Member States to ensure safe and healthy working conditions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In April 2023, the Government of Zambia approved an Occupational Health and Safety Policy. This is in order to prevent and reduce the number of occupational accidents and injury to health, arising out of and/or linked with or occurring in the course of work, by minimising occupational risks or eliminating risks at the source, which promotes principles on risk management and developing a preventive safety and health culture that includes information, consultation and training. The government, in consultation with social partners, adopted and operationalized an up-to-date national OSH policy addressing specific occupational hazards within an institutional framework of defined roles and responsibilities. The policy has strengthened the national OSH system by setting up, expanding the scope or reinforcing an existing infrastructure with a mandate on safety and health at work. Furthermore, as part of the implementation of the policy, and through stakeholders and tripartite partners consultation, the country has engaged in a South-to-South Cooperation initiative with China to enhance OSH skills and competence in mining sector through an OSH training needs assessment (TNA), training programme development and training delivery targeting seven selected metal mines. Other planned policy measures include promotion of gender-sensitive regulations in workplaces; mainstreaming of HIV and AIDS in occupational safety and health programmes; appropriate regulations for persons with disabilities; and enhanced integration of climate change programmes in occupational safety and  health services. The successful implementation of the national OSH policy will benefit the national competent authorities including the inspectorates through reinforced mandates and enhanced capacities, as well as the workers and enterprises including workers in informal sector, and other workers in situations of vulnerability, through extended protection and reduction of occupational injuries and diseases. In May 2022, the ILO held a consultative stakeholders&#x2019; meeting to review the draft policy and have it validated by the stakeholders. The ILO made inputs at the policy development meeting regarding good practices and OSH as a Fundamental Principle and Right at Work (FPPW). Further, the ILO also reviewed the draft policy and provided normative adjustments to the draft policy document. The final policy approved by the Government and launched on 28 April 2023 incorporates changes and suggestions made by the ILO. The ILO also conducted a Training Needs Assessment among the Mining sector that also informed the formulation of the approved policy by the Government. The ILO collaborated with the Zambia Chamber of Mines to undertake the Training Needs Assessment (TNA) in the Mining sector that formed part of the analysis that informed the approved Occupational Satefy and Health (OSH) Policy.</narrative>
   </description>
