   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.3. Increased institutional capacity and resilience of labour administrations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In May 2023, building on past interventions, the Government of Zambia developed a Strategic Compliance Plan on the Strategic Compliance Portal (online) that has a set of interventions including enforcement and is assisting the labour inspectorate in improving compliance. The plan is assisting the labour inspectorate in improving its delivery in collaboration with other actors with mandate and capacity to promote compliance in the security sector. The plan is assisting the labour inspectorate in improving its delivery in collaboration with other actors with mandate and capacity to promote compliance in the security sector. The Plan includes a combined set of interventions, including promotion of compliance and enforcement. A baseline inspection campaign, inspecting randomly selected duty holders.  In May 2022, as part of its participation on the subregional Strategic Compliance Portal (SCP) on private security. Zambia and the other four inspectorates reviewed data collected from the baseline campaign about both compliant and non-compliant duty holders to identify potential characteristics that might indicate a duty holder is likely to be non-compliant. The data analysis demonstrated that enterprises which were non-unionized, had at least one previous violation, labour dispute, or complaint, had high or medium employee turnover rates, provided indefinite term contracts, and worked primarily with either private or a mix of private and public clients were more likely to have non-compliance.  Following this data analysis and collection of key data points indicating non-compliance, Zambia designed a subsequent evidence-led campaign, shifting from a random selection approach to a more targeted approach to better target non-compliant enterprises, and saw a 37% increase in their ability to target noncompliant enterprises. In March 2023, the ILO convened a stakeholders&#x2019; consultative meeting with private security companies, trade unions covering the private security sector, employers&#x2019; organizations, and government line ministries and agencies. The meeting discussed the challenges (decent work deficits) identified from the inspections conducted in the private security firms. The decent work deficits identified included the following: 
1.	Failure to issue contracts of employment in writing. The Employment Code Act, No. 3 of 2019, Section 22(1) provides that-&#x201c;Where an employer engages an employee for a period of six months or more or for a number of working days equivalent to six months or more within a year, the contract of employment shall be in writing.&#x201d;
2.	Salary Underpayments/Failure to comply with prescribed Minimum Wages.
3.	Failure to pay salaries when due/delayed salaries. The Employment Code Act, No. 3 of 2019, Section 66(2) provides that- &#x201c;The wages of an employee shall fall due and be paid at regular intervals not being later than the fifth day following the date on which they fall due&#x201d;
4.	Failure to register employees for social security coverage with statutory bodies in charge of various social security schemes such as the National Pensions Scheme Authority (NAPSA), National Health Insurance Management Authority (NHIMA) and Workers Compensation Fund Control Board (WCFCB).
5.	Unfair and Unauthorised deductions from wages/salaries of employees.

As result of the meetings and social media campaign, there is an increased number of duty holders adopt compliant workplace practices in the security sector attributable to the Strategic Compliance Plan implemented by the Government of Zambia.</narrative>
   </description>
